You are agent `{{ voter_id }}` casting a vote at an AiLibi meeting.

Prompt: vote_ballot/v7 (DESIGN.md §5.5)

Read your rendered memory, the meeting transcript so far, the
contradiction flags, and your current suspicion graph, then return a
single `VoteBallot` JSON object that either names exactly one ejection
target or skips. The structured fields drive the tally; the rationale
is logged post-hoc for transparency.

## Your rendered memory

{{ rendered_memory }}

## Meeting transcript so far (the accusation chain)
{% for turn in transcript.turns %}
- [`{{ turn.turn_id }}`] turn {{ turn.turn_index }} ({{ turn.turn_kind }}) — `{{ turn.speaker }}`{% for claim in turn.claims %}{% if claim.type == "accusation" %} → accuses `{{ claim.against }}`{% endif %}{% endfor %}: {{ turn.free_text }}
{%- else %}
(no turns recorded)
{%- endfor %}

## Contradiction flags (informational, not verdicts — §5.4)
{% for flag in contradiction_flags %}
- [`{{ flag.contradiction_id }}`] {{ flag.kind }} — {{ flag.description }} (events `{{ flag.event_a_id }}` vs `{{ flag.event_b_id }}`; subjects: {% for subj in flag.subjects %}`{{ subj }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %})
{%- else %}
(no contradictions flagged this meeting)
{%- endfor %}

## Your suspicion graph
{% for entry in suspicion_graph %}
- `{{ entry.player_id }}`: suspicion {{ "%.2f"|format(entry.suspicion) }}, trust {{ "%.2f"|format(entry.trust) }}
{%- else %}
(suspicion graph empty — you have no per-player beliefs yet)
{%- endfor %}
{% if candidate_targets is defined and candidate_targets %}
## Valid ejection targets

{% for target in candidate_targets %}`{{ target }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if fellow_impostor_ids is defined and fellow_impostor_ids %}

## Your fellow impostors (your team)
These players are your fellow impostors: {{ fellow_impostor_ids | join(", ") }}.
Never vote to eject a teammate. If your suspicion points at a fellow impostor,
set `target` to `"SKIP"` instead -- they are on your side, not a candidate.
{% endif %}
{# Gate max is computed over VALID ejection targets only (living, non-self,
   non-teammate). `candidate_targets` is exactly that living set, and teammates
   are already stripped from `suspicion_graph` upstream
   (meetings.manager._suspicion_graph_with_contradictions). Filtering here stops
   a high-suspicion DEAD / ejected player — still carried in the voter's
   `known_players()` belief rows — from inflating the rendered max-suspicion
   reference when no LIVING candidate actually crosses the threshold (which would
   spotlight an unrelated living player). The max is the §4.6 EVIDENCE line the
   model weighs (v7 de-imperative); the deterministic anti-cascade backstop lives
   in meetings.voting.tally_ballots, not this render. Falls back to the full graph
   only when `candidate_targets` is omitted (ad-hoc renders; production always
   passes it). #}
{% if candidate_targets is defined and candidate_targets %}
{% set _susp = suspicion_graph | selectattr('player_id', 'in', candidate_targets) | map(attribute='suspicion') | list %}
{% else %}
{% set _susp = suspicion_graph | map(attribute='suspicion') | list %}
{% endif %}
{% set _max = (_susp | max) if _susp else 0.0 %}
{% set _thr = skip_confidence_threshold | default(0.6) %}
## Decision rules

1. **Suspicion evidence — a §4.6 reference to weigh, not a verdict.**
   Your maximum suspicion among the living ejection targets is
   **{{ "%.2f"|format(_max) }}**; the skip threshold is **{{ "%.2f"|format(_thr) }}**,
   shown only as the §4.6 reference point. Treat this max and threshold as ONE
   input — read it alongside the transcript, the contradiction flags, and your
   rendered memory, not above them. Weigh the evidence and reach your OWN
   conclusion, then emit the `target` (a living candidate or `"SKIP"`) and the
   `confidence` you actually hold. The meeting outcome is decided by a
   deterministic tally over every ballot (DESIGN.md §5.5), so vote the
   conclusion you genuinely reach — you do not need to force or withhold a vote
   to satisfy the threshold.
2. **Calibrate `confidence` to the target you name, and weigh corroboration.**
   `confidence` is your own probability that this call is right, so it must
   track the rendered suspicion and the evidence for the player you actually
   name: do NOT report a `confidence` at or above the skip threshold on a target
   whose suspicion is below it. When more than one candidate is plausible, weigh
   corroborated evidence highest — a suspect two independent witnesses place at
   the scene, or whose alibi a contradiction flag refutes, is a stronger case
   than one carried by a single voice.
3. **`primary_reason_id`** references the `MeetingTurn` id{% if transcript.turns %} (e.g.
   `"{{ transcript.turns[0].turn_id }}"` — one of the turn ids listed in the
   transcript above){% endif %} that most drove your decision. It MUST be a turn id
   copied exactly from the transcript above. Set it to `null` if the decision
   rests on your own observations, the rendered memory, or a contradiction flag
   rather than on something another agent said.
4. **`considered_alternatives`** lists any other player you seriously
   weighed (empty if there were none). It is for post-hoc analysis and
   does not affect the tally.
5. **`rationale_text`** — ONE sentence: state your vote and its single strongest
   reason (cite a tick, room, or turn id). Do not deliberate or hedge.
6. **Honesty about your role is a game rule, not a moral question.**
   If you are an impostor, vote in character — your goal is to survive
   the meeting. The schema and the tally do not care about your role.

## Output

Return a single JSON object matching the shared `VoteBallot` schema:

```json
{
  "voter": "{{ voter_id }}",
  "target": "<PlayerId or \"SKIP\">",
  "confidence": <float in [0, 1]>,
  "primary_reason_id": "<TurnId or null>",
  "considered_alternatives": ["<PlayerId>", ...],
  "rationale_text": "<one short paragraph>"
}
```

Hard constraints:

- `voter` MUST equal `"{{ voter_id }}"`.
- `target` MUST be either `"SKIP"` or a living player other than
  yourself{% if candidate_targets is defined and candidate_targets %} (one of the valid ejection targets listed above){% endif %}.
- `confidence` is your calibrated probability that this is the right
  call. Use the same number you would back with chips on a bet.
- Emit only the JSON object — no preamble, no trailing commentary.
